  "worked_solution": "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma .$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=174\\,\\text{ft},\\,n=25,\\,s=4\\,\\text{ft}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{24,0.05}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{24,0.05})=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=25-1=24$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14203146/assets/52426e7ca1868359b5ed5c97ba1fabeaebb22b73393918d5b9031c8c6320a300.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{24,0.05}=1.711:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $23$ | $\\,1.319\\,$ | $\\,1.714\\,$ | $\\,2.069\\,$ | $\\,2.500\\,$ | $\\,2.807\\,$ |\n| $24$ | $\\,1.318\\,$ | $1.711$ | $\\,2.064\\,$ | $\\,2.492\\,$ | $\\,2.797\\,$ |\n| $25$ | $\\,1.316\\,$ | $\\,1.708\\,$ | $\\,2.060\\,$ | $\\,2.485\\,$ | $\\,2.787\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}174 & \\pm 1.711\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{25}}\\,\\text{ft} \\\\ 174 & \\pm 1.369\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
